.TH NETMON-UNINSTALL 1 "November 2026" "NetMon 1.1.22~beta4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netmon-uninstall \- guided removal utility for NetMon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netmon-uninstall
.SH DESCRIPTION
The desktop entry Manage NetMon (NetMon verwalten) opens a shared graphical
window for installation/update, removal keeping personal data, or removal
deleting personal data. Opening this window changes nothing. Selecting an
operation opens the appropriate terminal assistant with its existing prompts
and final confirmation. The former separate installation desktop entry is hidden.
.PP
.B netmon-uninstall
opens an interactive terminal workflow with three separate operations:
update from a local DEB, uninstall while retaining user data (the default),
or uninstall with explicitly confirmed user-data deletion.
User data is not removed by the Debian package's normal removal scripts.
.PP
The graphical entry passes --mode keep or --mode delete to preselect the
removal operation. This does not bypass its final confirmation.
.SH UPDATE
The separate netmon-install command provides the shared installation/update
assistant, including first installation. Its standalone netmon-install.py copy
also works before NetMon is installed or with versions lacking this menu.
Run that starter without sudo; it requests privileges only for package installation.
Direct APT installation does not invoke an interactive backup dialog.
.PP
Supply the absolute path of a trusted local netmon DEB. The utility requires
a higher package version and the native or all architecture. It copies the
DEB into a private root-owned staging directory before reviewing its metadata.
A full versioned backup is created and every payload hash is checked using
the invoking account's permissions. The default backup parent is
NetMon-Sicherungen beside the active NetMon document directory.
Backup failure aborts the update. The final summary shows the package version,
package hash and backup result and requires confirmation.
.PP
APT installs the staged package directly with --no-remove and
--no-install-recommends. No uninstall, profile cleanup, firewall reset or
optional-package removal is performed by this update operation. A failed
installation is reported; no automatic restore or package rollback is attempted.
This is a local-package workflow, not a downloader or publisher-signature verifier.
.SH RETAIN OR DELETE USER DATA
Retaining data forbids all browser, user VPN-file, system WireGuard-profile and
NetworkManager VPN-deletion choices. Settings, favorites, slots and profile files
remain available for reinstallation. Optional firewall reset and dependency
removal remain separately confirmed operations.
.PP
The delete-data operation previews exact file targets before the final
confirmation. Secure Browser removal remains a separate explicit choice in
this operation. Backups outside the listed NetMon data directories are retained.
.SH PACKAGE AND USER TARGETS
The package target is fixed to netmon. Caller overrides of NETMON_PACKAGE,
HOME and XDG_DOCUMENTS_DIR do not select privileged removal targets.
The invoking account is checked against the system account database; its home
directory is obtained from that database. User-file cleanup runs with that
account's permissions in a clean environment. Root-account user data is retained.
Documents and Dokumente below that home are considered automatically, together
with a saved XDG document directory below the same home. The saved XDG setting
is read without inherited XDG environment overrides and without root privileges.
External or redirected document directories are reported and retained for
separate manual review.
The final summary displays the package, account, home and requested file targets.
Symlink redirects and paths outside the verified home are refused.
.SH WIREGUARD OWNERSHIP
Before package removal or deconfiguration, NetMon stops its own wg-quick
tunnels using protected creation receipts and the saved original teardown
configuration. Changed identities or unsafe teardown data abort removal;
the helper and evidence remain installed. Package upgrades do not disconnect
these tunnels. Unproven legacy and foreign interfaces are retained and require
separate administrator review. Saved VPN profile files are not deleted.
.PP
Automatic NetworkManager cleanup requires a protected NetMon creation receipt,
an unchanged root-owned keyfile, and a matching WireGuard UUID. The current
names and exact UUIDs are displayed for separate confirmation. User profile
JSON, connection names and user-supplied ownership markers grant no authority.
Connections from older installations or changed profiles are retained. There
is no automatic adoption or force-cleanup option; an administrator must review
and separately approve any manual deletion by exact UUID.
User VPN-file cleanup retains system WireGuard configurations. The separate
test-system option to delete all WireGuard configurations is intentionally
destructive and must not be used as a NetMon ownership check.
.SH WARNING
Review every prompt before confirming optional cleanup. WireGuard, DNS,
NetworkManager, and user-data cleanup choices can affect system configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR netmon (1),
.BR dpkg (1),
.BR apt (8)
